Q: Can I use Canvas.Pen.Style when Canvas.Pen.Width &gt; 1? A: <p>No, non-solid line styles are only supported with a line width of 1.</p>
<p>The VCL's <code>TCanvas</code> is based on Windows GDI, and this is a limitation of the GDI pen. For instance, at the documentation for <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wingdi/nf-wingdi-createpen" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CreatePen</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The pen is dashed. This style is valid only when the pen width is one
or less in device units.</p>
<p>[...]</p>
<p>CreatePen returns a pen with the specified width but with the PS_SOLID style if you specify a width greater than one for the following styles: PS_DASH, PS_DOT, PS_DASHDOT, PS_DASHDOTDOT.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Personally, when I need to draw in a window with a thicker non-solid pen, I therefore use GDI+ instead of GDI. (The only reason I have ever used GDI+ is to achieve precisely this.)</p>
<p>If you want to be really fancy, you could also use Direct2D (or even OpenGL, but then you are really overkilling it).</p>
<h1>GDI+ Sample</h1>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/oyyrMTA4.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/oyyrMTA4.png" alt="Output of the program: a white window with two thick, dash-dottted diagonal lines." /></a></p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>unit Unit1;

interface

uses
  Winapi.Windows, Winapi.Messages, System.SysUtils, System.Variants, System.Classes, Vcl.Graphics,
  Vcl.Controls, Vcl.Forms, Vcl.Dialogs, GDIPAPI, GDIPOBJ;

type
  TForm1 = class(TForm)
    procedure FormPaint(Sender: TObject);
    procedure FormResize(Sender: TObject);
  private
  protected
  public
    procedure WMEraseBkgnd(var Message: TWMEraseBkgnd); message WM_ERASEBKGND;
  end;

var
  Form1: TForm1;

implementation

{$R *.dfm}

procedure TForm1.FormPaint(Sender: TObject);

  function PC(const AColor: TColor): Cardinal; inline;
  // Creates a GDI+ color value from a TColor
  begin
    Result := $FF000000 or GetRValue(AColor) shl 16 or GetGValue(AColor) shl 8 or GetBValue(AColor);
  end;

begin

  Canvas.Brush.Color := clWhite;
  Canvas.FillRect(ClientRect);

  var G := TGPGraphics.Create(Canvas.Handle);
  try
    var p := TGPPen.Create(PC(clRed), 5);
    try
      p.SetDashStyle(DashStyleDashDotDot);
      g.DrawLine(p, 0, 0, ClientWidth, ClientHeight);
      g.DrawLine(p, ClientWidth, 0, 0, ClientHeight);
    finally
      p.Free;
    end;
  finally
    G.Free;
  end;

end;

procedure TForm1.FormResize(Sender: TObject);
begin
  Invalidate;
end;

procedure TForm1.WMEraseBkgnd(var Message: TWMEraseBkgnd);
begin
  Message.Result := 1;
end;

end.

</code></pre>

Q: TToolbar stops displaying button captions after a while A: <p>I had a similar issue with a crash, due to my application running embedded in an other application.</p>
<p>Problem was simple: In the toolbar I have a &quot;Record&quot; button. When pressed, the application records an image stream, and the button flashes red every half second. This is done simply with a TTimer, which changes the ImageIndex:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code> void __fastcall TFMain :: OnFlashTimer ( TObject *Sender ) {

    if ( tbRecord-&gt;ImageIndex == 5 ) {
        tbRecord-&gt;ImageIndex = 6;
    }
    else {
        tbRecord-&gt;ImageIndex = 5;
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>But changing the ImageIndex in one button leads to the recreation of the whole toolbar. Not only that, the string table used gets filled with the same captions from all the elements over and over again, instead of reusing them. Which leads to the code in the other answer, which was added in the VCL to combat that problem:</p>
<pre><code>  // If more than 2^16 strings are TB_ADDSTRING-ed to the tool bar's string
  // pool, the Windows API assumes iString is a pointer to a null terminated
  // string, not an index in the string pool.  Therefore we have to recreate
  // the toolbar to reset the string pool so the strings display propperly.
  if Button.iString &gt;= 65536 then
    RecreateWnd;
</code></pre>
<p>I mean, come on, Embarcadero, index error for an array? Maybe the spelling error in the comment was a hint... What the other answer missed as well is that the if-clause should check for</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>if Button.iString &gt;= 65535 then
    RecreateWnd;
</code></pre>
<p>Otherwise as the comment suggests, the index is interpretet as a pointer, which leads to an access-violation. As a stand-alone application this gives you garbage strings, because a random memory is read. In my case, as I was embedded in an other application, it leads to a crash, because the of an access violation.</p>
<p>The bug is present in RAD Studio XE7. I checked in XE12, and there it is fixed with the correct limit. So somewhere inbetween it was corrected quietly.</p>
<p>So solution one is to copy and fix the ComCtrls.pas and link it in the project.</p>
<p>Another solution is to set the image directly with a message, then the whole deleting, recreating, dumping strings in the pool, garbage code is not executed:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code> void __fastcall TFMain :: OnFlashTimer ( TObject *Sender ) {

    static bool bBlink = false;

    TBBUTTONINFO bi;
    memset(&amp;bi, 0, sizeof(bi));
    bi.cbSize = sizeof(TBBUTTONINFO);

    bi.dwMask = TBIF_IMAGE | TBIF_BYINDEX;

    if ( bBlink ) {
        bi.iImage = 6;
    }
    else {
        bi.iImage = 5;
    }
    bBlink = !bBlink;

    SendMessage( toolbar-&gt;Handle, TB_SETBUTTONINFO, (WPARAM)tbRecord-&gt;Index, (LPARAM)&amp;bi);

    toolbar-&gt;Invalidate();

</code></pre>
